<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate the Semiperimeter of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>Add the three sides of a right triangle, then divide the total by two.</p>
  <p>That is the entire calculation, and this calculator performs it for any right triangle once you enter the two legs and the hypotenuse. Three side measurements are needed because the triangle has three edges, and the hypotenuse must be included: it is one of those three edges even though it never touches the right angle. The total is divided by two because the semiperimeter is defined as exactly half the full perimeter, a quantity that appears again and again in triangle geometry.</p>
  <p>A quick example shows the idea. Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[s=\\frac{12}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6\\text{ units}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The full perimeter is 12 units, so its semiperimeter is 6 units. Notice that the semiperimeter is a linear measurement, not an area measurement: it is a length, reported in metres, feet, or plain units, never in square units. It becomes useful whenever a formula asks for half the perimeter, which happens in Heron’s formula for area, in the inradius relationship, and in several geometric proofs where the full perimeter would only get in the way.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding the geometry -->
<section id="sec-geometry" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Before any division by two happens, the three sides must be labelled correctly. The symbols below stay fixed through the whole guide, and the calculator uses the same names.</p>

  <h3>Definition of a Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>A right triangle is a triangle containing one interior angle measuring exactly 90 degrees. It has three vertices, three sides, two acute angles, and one right angle, usually marked with a small square. The two sides that form the right angle are the legs; the side opposite the right angle is the hypotenuse. Because the angles of any triangle add to 180 degrees, <span set:html={rich('[[A+B+C=180^\\circ]]')} />, and with <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} /> it follows that <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A+B=90^\\circ}]]]')} />: the two acute angles are complementary, so knowing one determines the other. Getting this picture right matters because the semiperimeter formula treats every side as a boundary edge, and a mislabelled side corrupts the total before the division even starts.</p>

  <h3>What Are the Two Legs?</h3>
  <p>Leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the two sides that meet at the right-angle vertex, perpendicular to each other. Either one can be drawn horizontal or vertical depending on the triangle’s orientation; what defines them is the 90-degree corner they form, not their direction on the page. They differ from the hypotenuse in position and in length: each leg touches the right angle, and each leg is shorter than the hypotenuse. In semiperimeter work their role is simple: both are boundary edges, so both enter the sum that gets halved.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, always the longest side of the triangle. It never forms the right angle; it spans across from it, joining the far ends of the two legs. It differs from the legs in both position and length: the legs meet at 90 degrees, while the hypotenuse meets each leg at an acute angle. It completes the triangular boundary, which is why it must be included in the semiperimeter calculation. When both legs are known, the Pythagorean theorem recovers it: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}]]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>How to Identify the Correct Side Labels</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the two sides meeting at 90 degrees, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is opposite the right angle. In a non-degenerate right triangle <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the longest side. The right-angle marker tells you which side is <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />: it is the one that does not touch that vertex. A quick ordering check helps: sort the three numbers, and the largest must play the role of <span set:html={rich('[[c]].')} /> If the largest side does not sit opposite the square corner, the labels are wrong, and the semiperimeter built on them will be wrong too.</p>

  <h3>Why Does Semiperimeter Depend on All Three Sides?</h3>
  <p>Semiperimeter is derived from the complete perimeter, and the complete perimeter counts every edge of the boundary. Even though <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are perpendicular, the hypotenuse remains part of the triangle’s outside boundary: it is the edge that closes the shape. Excluding <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> does not give a shorter semiperimeter; it gives a different, wrong quantity. For <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, leaving out the hypotenuse produces <span set:html={rich('[[(3+4)/2=3.5]]')} />, while the true semiperimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[(3+4+5)/2=6]].')} /> The missing 2.5 units are the hypotenuse’s share, and no correct formula can skip it.</p>

