<!-- Section 3: What semiperimeter means -->
<section id="sec-meaning" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Semiperimeter of a Right Triangle?</h2>

  <h3>Definition of Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>Semiperimeter is exactly half the full perimeter of a triangle. If <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> is the complete distance around the triangle, then the semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{P}{2}}]]]')} />. For a right triangle the full perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[P=a+b+c]]')} />, so the definition becomes <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}}]]]')} />. All three side lengths are added before the division: the sum collects the whole boundary, and the division by two halves it. Nothing is left out and nothing is counted twice.</p>

  <h3>Why Is It Called Semiperimeter?</h3>
  <p>The name explains itself once it is split apart. <em>Semi</em> means half, and <em>perimeter</em> means the complete boundary length. Put together, semiperimeter means half the perimeter. The letter <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is the standard mathematical symbol, and it appears in many triangle formulas precisely because halved boundary lengths turn up so often. When a formula asks for <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />, it is asking for this one number: half of <span set:html={rich('[[a+b+c]].')} /></p>

  <h3>Does Semiperimeter Represent a Physical Half-Boundary Path?</h3>
  <p>This is a subtle point worth getting right. The semiperimeter is half the numerical total of the three side lengths, but it does not necessarily describe a continuous physical route covering half the triangle’s boundary. Walking 6 units along the edges of a 3-4-5 triangle, for example, could mean walking the two legs (3 + 4 = 7 is already too far) or one leg plus part of the hypotenuse; there is no single natural halfway path. The semiperimeter is primarily a derived geometric measurement, a convenient intermediate value that formulas use, rather than a distance you would pace out on the ground.</p>

  <h3>Is Semiperimeter a Length or an Area?</h3>
  <p>Semiperimeter is a one-dimensional measurement: a length. If the side lengths are in metres, <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is in metres; in centimetres, it is in centimetres; in feet, in feet; in inches, in inches. It must never be expressed in square units. The division by two changes the size of the number, not its dimension: halving a length still leaves a length. Confusing <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> with an area is one of the most common beginner errors, and keeping the units straight is the simplest way to avoid it.</p>

  <h3>Is Semiperimeter Always Greater Than the Longest Side?</h3>
  <p>Yes, for every valid non-degenerate triangle, and the triangle inequality shows why. Every triangle satisfies <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]]')} />: the two shorter sides together exceed the longest. Add <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> to both sides and <span set:html={rich('[[a+b+c>2c]]')} /> follows; divide by two and the result is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s>c}]]]')} />. For a right triangle this means the semiperimeter is always greater than the hypotenuse. For 3-4-5, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} /> exceeds <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]].')} /> This makes a handy sanity check: if your computed semiperimeter is smaller than the hypotenuse, the side labels or the arithmetic are wrong.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: The formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Semiperimeter Formula</h2>
  <p>Two forms of the same formula are used, depending on what is known. The compact form starts from the full perimeter:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{P}{2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The expanded form starts from the three sides:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}}]]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Understanding Each Variable</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is the semiperimeter, the answer being computed. <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> is the full perimeter, the complete boundary length. <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the first leg and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> the second leg, the two perpendicular sides. <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse, the longest side, opposite the right angle. Never interchange <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />: one is the whole boundary, the other is half of it.</p>

  <h3>Why Do We Divide by Two?</h3>
  <p>The division is what makes the quantity a <em>semi</em>perimeter rather than a perimeter. Take <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]].')} /> First calculate the complete perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=3+4+5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[P=12]].')} /> Then divide by two: <span set:html={rich('[[s=12/2]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6}]]]')} />. Mathematically, the division rescales the boundary total to the half-value that formulas like Heron’s expect. Skipping it answers a different question: the full perimeter, not the semiperimeter.</p>

  <h3>Why Can’t We Use Only the Two Legs?</h3>
  <p>Because the legs alone are not the whole boundary. The incorrect approach <span set:html={rich('[[s=(3+4)/2]]')} /> gives 3.5, a number with no geometric meaning for this triangle: it ignores the hypotenuse, which is a real edge of the shape. The correct approach adds all three sides first: <span set:html={rich('[[s=(3+4+5)/2]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6}]]]')} />. A tempting shortcut is to halve each side and then add, <span set:html={rich('[[1.5+2+2.5=6]]')} />: the arithmetic happens to work here because division distributes over addition, but it obscures the formula’s structure and invites mistakes. Add first, then divide once.</p>

  <h3>How Are Perimeter and Semiperimeter Related?</h3>
  <p>They are the same measurement at two scales. Doubling the semiperimeter recovers the perimeter, and halving the perimeter recovers the semiperimeter:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=2s}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{P}{2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Either value can be recovered when the other is known. If a problem states the perimeter is 30, the semiperimeter is immediately 15; if a calculation yields <span set:html={rich('[[s=20]]')} />, the full perimeter is 40. This two-way relationship is also a verification tool: after computing <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> from the sides, doubling it must reproduce the side sum.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-calculator" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Semiperimeter Calculator</h2>
  <p>The calculator on this page has one job: it takes the three side lengths of a right triangle and returns the semiperimeter, showing each step of the work. The steps below match the existing interface exactly.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 1: Locate the Semiperimeter Calculator.</strong> It sits near the top of the page, below the H1 heading, with three labelled inputs.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2: Identify leg a.</strong> Pick one of the two sides forming the right angle. Either perpendicular side can be <span set:html={rich('[[a]].')} /></p>
  <p><strong>Step 3: Identify leg b.</strong> The other perpendicular side becomes <span set:html={rich('[[b]].')} /></p>
  <p><strong>Step 4: Identify hypotenuse c.</strong> The longest side, opposite the right angle, is <span set:html={rich('[[c]].')} /> Sorting the three measurements and placing the largest in this field prevents the most common input error.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 5: Enter the first leg.</strong> Type the value of <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> into the Leg a field.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 6: Enter the second leg.</strong> Type the value of <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> into the Leg b field.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 7: Enter the hypotenuse.</strong> Type the value of <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> into the Hypotenuse c field.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 8: Select appropriate measurement units.</strong> Each input has its own unit selector. All three sides must use compatible units before they are added; mixing metres with centimetres corrupts the sum.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 9: Click Calculate Semiperimeter.</strong> The tool adds the three sides and divides the total by two.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 10: Review the displayed semiperimeter.</strong> The result appears with its unit, for example <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6\\text{ units}}]]]')} /> for the 3-4-5 triangle.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 11: Check the solution steps.</strong> The calculator shows the substitution and the division, so you can follow the arithmetic line by line.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 12: Verify that the output is half the full perimeter.</strong> Double the result: for 3-4-5, <span set:html={rich('[[2\\times6=12]]')} />, which matches <span set:html={rich('[[3+4+5]].')} /></p>
  <p><strong>Step 13: Use Reset when starting a new calculation.</strong> The Reset button clears all three inputs so the next triangle starts from a clean state.</p>

  <h3>What Does Each Input Represent?</h3>
  <p>The three inputs correspond to the three boundary edges. Leg a and Leg b accept the two perpendicular sides; Hypotenuse c accepts the longest side. The calculator adds exactly what you enter and halves the total, so each field’s role is fixed: swapping a leg value into the hypotenuse field changes the sum and produces a wrong semiperimeter.</p>

  <h3>Why Are Measurement Units Important?</h3>
  <p>Semiperimeter is computed by adding three lengths, and addition requires compatible units. Adding 3 metres to 40 centimetres without conversion gives a meaningless total. Each input’s unit selector lets you declare the unit you typed; choose selectors that describe the real measurements so the addition combines like with like.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Result Display</h3>
  <p>The result display shows the semiperimeter with its linear unit. Behind it, the calculator performed exactly two operations: it added all three sides, then divided the total by two. No other computation is involved, and the tool does not calculate area or inradius on its own; those formulas are supporting explanations in this guide, not extra outputs of the calculator.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Solution Steps</h3>
  <p>The solution steps panel replays the calculation: the entered side values, their total, the division by two, and the final result. This visible working is the fastest way to catch a typo: if a step shows 4 where you meant 40, the error is obvious before the answer is used anywhere else.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Four fully worked examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Right Triangle Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>The four examples below keep the existing worked cases and expand their explanations. Each one substitutes the three sides, adds them, divides by two, and then verifies the answer.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3,\\;b=4,\\;c=5]]')} />, the formula is <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]].')} /> Substitute: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}]].')} /> Add: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{12}{2}]].')} /> The final result is <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Verification: <span set:html={rich('[[P=2s=12]]')} />, which matches the direct sum <span set:html={rich('[[3+4+5]].')} /> The result is correct because every boundary edge was counted exactly once before the division.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5,\\;b=12,\\;c=13]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{5+12+13}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{30}{2}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=15\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. The full perimeter is 30, and half of 30 is 15. As a check, <span set:html={rich('[[5^2+12^2=13^2]]')} /> confirms the right angle, so the side labels are trustworthy.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: 8-15-17 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=8,\\;b=15,\\;c=17]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{8+15+17}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{40}{2}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=20\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Notice the pattern across the examples: as the triangle grows, the semiperimeter grows in step with the sides, always exactly half the perimeter.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: Decimal Side Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=2.5,\\;b=6,\\;c=6.5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{2.5+6+6.5}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{15}{2}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=7.5\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Decimal values do not change the formula: the same add-then-divide sequence applies. The Pythagorean check <span set:html={rich('[[2.5^2+6^2=6.5^2]]')} /> confirms these sides genuinely form a right triangle.</p>

