<!-- Section 7: Twelve practical real-world examples -->
<section id="sec-practical" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Applications of Right Triangle Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>One caution before the examples: the semiperimeter is not normally the quantity used to purchase half the edging or fencing around a triangular object. Its real role is as a geometric intermediate measurement, the stepping stone to inradius, area checks, and design calculations. Each example below is a simplified right-triangular geometric model, and each computes the semiperimeter alongside the full perimeter, the area, and the inradius so the relationships stay visible.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: Triangular Window With a Circular Decorative Insert</h3>
  <p>A triangular window model has <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ m},\\;b=4\\text{ m},\\;c=5\\text{ m}]].')} /> Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}=6\\text{ m}]]')} />; full perimeter <span set:html={rich('[[P=12\\text{ m}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6\\text{ m}^2]]')} /> and inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{K}{s}=\\frac{6}{6}=1\\text{ m}]].')} /> The semiperimeter is what makes the inradius computable: it converts three side lengths into the single number that links area to the radius of the circle tangent to all three sides of the idealized triangular model.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: Metal Support Bracket With a Circular Clearance</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular bracket has <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\text{ cm},\\;b=12\\text{ cm},\\;c=13\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{5+12+13}{2}=15\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[P=30\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{5\\times12}{2}=30\\text{ cm}^2]]')} /> gives inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{30}{15}=2\\text{ cm}]].')} /> This value describes the incircle radius in the idealized triangle and can guide geometric clearance studies for a circular cutout. It does not, by itself, determine structural safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: Roof Gable With a Circular Vent Design</h3>
  <p>A simplified right-triangular roof-end model has <span set:html={rich('[[a=6\\text{ ft},\\;b=8\\text{ ft},\\;c=10\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{6+8+10}{2}=12\\text{ ft}]]')} />; perimeter <span set:html={rich('[[P=24\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{6\\times8}{2}=24\\text{ ft}^2]]')} /> and inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{24}{12}=2\\text{ ft}]].')} /> The semiperimeter turns the gable’s three edge lengths into the incircle radius of the simplified model. Real vent installation needs separate building and clearance measurements beyond this geometry.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: Triangular Garden Design</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular garden model has <span set:html={rich('[[a=7\\text{ m},\\;b=24\\text{ m},\\;c=25\\text{ m}]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{7+24+25}{2}=28\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[P=56\\text{ m}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{7\\times24}{2}=84\\text{ m}^2]]')} /> yields inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{84}{28}=3\\text{ m}]].')} /> The incircle is the largest circle that fits inside the idealized triangle while touching all three sides, useful for placing a circular bed or fountain at the design stage.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: CNC Triangular Fabrication Panel</h3>
  <p>A triangular panel has <span set:html={rich('[[a=8\\text{ cm},\\;b=15\\text{ cm},\\;c=17\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{8+15+17}{2}=20\\text{ cm}]]')} />; perimeter <span set:html={rich('[[P=40\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{8\\times15}{2}=60\\text{ cm}^2]]')} /> and inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{60}{20}=3\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Semiperimeter and inradius serve here as geometric reference values in CAD modeling, for example when positioning a circular feature relative to the panel’s edges.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Triangular Sign With a Circular Emblem</h3>
  <p>A sign panel has <span set:html={rich('[[a=9\\text{ in},\\;b=12\\text{ in},\\;c=15\\text{ in}]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{9+12+15}{2}=18\\text{ in}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[P=36\\text{ in}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{9\\times12}{2}=54\\text{ in}^2]]')} /> gives inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{54}{18}=3\\text{ in}]].')} /> The inradius provides a mathematical reference for the maximum inscribed circular emblem, subject to the actual margins and mounting constraints of the real sign.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Industrial Triangular Plate</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular plate has <span set:html={rich('[[a=9\\text{ cm},\\;b=40\\text{ cm},\\;c=41\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{9+40+41}{2}=45\\text{ cm}]]')} />; perimeter <span set:html={rich('[[P=90\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{9\\times40}{2}=180\\text{ cm}^2]]')} /> and inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{180}{45}=4\\text{ cm}]].')} /> The semiperimeter supports geometric calculations for an idealized industrial panel, such as sizing a circular boss that must stay clear of all three edges.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: Triangular Stained-Glass Design</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular glass section has <span set:html={rich('[[a=12\\text{ in},\\;b=16\\text{ in},\\;c=20\\text{ in}]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{12+16+20}{2}=24\\text{ in}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[P=48\\text{ in}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{12\\times16}{2}=96\\text{ in}^2]]')} /> yields inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{96}{24}=4\\text{ in}]].')} /> The relationship is direct: the semiperimeter halves the boundary, and dividing the area by that half-boundary gives the inscribed circle’s radius for the design layout.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: Surveyed Triangular Land Section</h3>
  <p>A simplified right-triangular parcel model has <span set:html={rich('[[a=20\\text{ m},\\;b=21\\text{ m},\\;c=29\\text{ m}]].')} /> Semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{20+21+29}{2}=35\\text{ m}]]')} />; perimeter <span set:html={rich('[[P=70\\text{ m}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{20\\times21}{2}=210\\text{ m}^2]]')} /> and inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{210}{35}=6\\text{ m}]].')} /> In a simplified land-surveying model, the semiperimeter serves as a compact input to area and inradius checks, for instance when verifying a computed parcel area against Heron’s formula.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Architectural Triangular Frame</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular architectural frame has <span set:html={rich('[[a=12\\text{ m},\\;b=35\\text{ m},\\;c=37\\text{ m}]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{12+35+37}{2}=42\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[P=84\\text{ m}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{12\\times35}{2}=210\\text{ m}^2]]')} /> gives inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{210}{42}=5\\text{ m}]].')} /> This value can support mathematical modeling of a circular feature inside the triangular frame, such as a round window centred on the incenter.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Large Triangular Floor Design</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular floor section has <span set:html={rich('[[a=28\\text{ m},\\;b=45\\text{ m},\\;c=53\\text{ m}]].')} /> Semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{28+45+53}{2}=63\\text{ m}]]')} />; perimeter <span set:html={rich('[[P=126\\text{ m}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{28\\times45}{2}=630\\text{ m}^2]]')} /> and inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{630}{63}=10\\text{ m}]].')} /> Here the semiperimeter connects the side measurements with the incircle geometry of the model: one number bridges the boundary and the largest fittable circle.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: Industrial Triangular Support Plate</h3>
  <p>A large right-triangular plate has <span set:html={rich('[[a=33\\text{ cm},\\;b=56\\text{ cm},\\;c=65\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{33+56+65}{2}=77\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[P=154\\text{ cm}]].')} /> Area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{33\\times56}{2}=924\\text{ cm}^2]]')} /> yields inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{924}{77}=12\\text{ cm}]].')} /> These measurements feed a geometric model for a circular fitting or internal design feature, with the semiperimeter as the intermediate value that unlocks the inradius.</p>

