<!-- Section 8: Semiperimeter and inradius -->
<section id="sec-inradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Is Semiperimeter Related to the Inradius of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>This is one of the most important sections of the article, because the inradius is the main reason the semiperimeter exists as a named quantity. The incircle is the largest circle that fits inside a triangle while touching all three sides, and its radius is called the inradius, represented by <span set:html={rich('[[r]].')} /></p>

  <h3>Area and Semiperimeter Relationship</h3>
  <p>For any triangle, the area, the inradius, and the semiperimeter are linked by a single compact formula:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=rs}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>where <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> is the triangle area, <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> is the inradius, and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is the semiperimeter. Rearranged, it solves for the inradius:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{K}{s}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Knowing the semiperimeter and the area therefore lets you calculate the inradius directly: divide the area by the semiperimeter. This is the practical payoff of the semiperimeter. It compresses three side lengths into one number that, together with the area, reveals the inscribed circle.</p>

  <h3>Special Inradius Formula for Right Triangles</h3>
  <p>For a right triangle the area is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]]')} /> and the semiperimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]].')} /> Substituting both into <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{K}{s}]]')} /> gives:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[r=\\frac{ab/2}{(a+b+c)/2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Simplifying:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{ab}{a+b+c}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>There is also an equivalent right-triangle formula that avoids the area entirely:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>These two expressions are equivalent for a right triangle because of the Pythagorean relationship: the algebra collapses once <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> is applied. Either form gives the same inradius; the second is often quicker by hand.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked 3-4-5 Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3,\\;b=4,\\;c=5]]')} />: semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}=6]]')} />, area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6]]')} />, inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{6}{6}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=1}]]]')} />. Verify with the shortcut: <span set:html={rich('[[r=\\frac{3+4-5}{2}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=1}]]]')} />. Both methods produce the same answer because they are the same mathematics written two ways: one passes through the area, the other cancels it out algebraically.</p>

  <h3>Why Is Semiperimeter Important for Incircle Calculations?</h3>
  <p>The semiperimeter is the bridge between the triangle’s boundary and its inscribed circle. Designers use it when sizing circular clearances inside triangular parts, when placing inscribed circular features in geometric design, when dimensioning engineering diagrams, and when working through mathematical proofs that relate area to radius. The inradius also connects back to area verification: if <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> do not satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} />, something in the chain is wrong. This calculator does not compute the inradius automatically; it supplies the semiperimeter, and the formulas above take it from there.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('semiperimeter-incircle.svg')} alt="3-4-5 right triangle with incircle of radius 1; semiperimeter s = 6, area K = 6, r = K/s" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The incircle touches all three sides; with s = 6 and K = 6, the inradius r = K / s = 1.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: Semiperimeter and Heron's formula -->
<section id="sec-heron" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How Is Semiperimeter Used in Heron’s Formula?</h2>
  <p>Heron’s formula calculates the area of a triangle from its three side lengths, without requiring a separately supplied height. The semiperimeter is its central ingredient: the formula is written in terms of <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> and the differences between <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> and each side.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> is the triangle area, <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is the semiperimeter, and <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> are the three side lengths.</p>

  <h3>Why Does Heron’s Formula Use Semiperimeter?</h3>
  <p>The semiperimeter is calculated first because it centres the expression: each factor <span set:html={rich('[[s-a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s-b]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s-c]]')} /> measures how much the half-perimeter exceeds one side. Multiplying those three excesses by <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> itself and taking the square root converts boundary information into area. The formula works for any valid triangle, not only right triangles, which is why the semiperimeter earns its keep as a general geometric tool rather than a right-triangle curiosity.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked 3-4-5 Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3,\\;b=4,\\;c=5]]')} />, first calculate the semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}=6]].')} /> Substitute into Heron’s formula: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{6(6-3)(6-4)(6-5)}]].')} /> Simplify: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{6\\times3\\times2\\times1}]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\sqrt{36}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>Verify the Result Using the Right Triangle Area Formula</h3>
  <p>The direct right-triangle area formula is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{ab}{2}]].')} /> Substituting: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Both formulas produce the same area, which is exactly the point: Heron’s formula, built on the semiperimeter, independently confirms the simpler calculation.</p>

  <h3>Why Is This Useful?</h3>
  <p>Right triangles have a simpler direct area formula, so Heron’s formula is rarely the fastest route to the area. Its value lies in checking area calculations, in understanding how side lengths alone determine area, in solving side-length-based problems where no height is given, and in verifying mathematical solutions through an independent path. Whenever two unrelated formulas agree on the area, confidence in the answer rises sharply.</p>

