<!-- Section 10: Semiperimeter vs perimeter -->
<section id="sec-vs-perimeter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Difference Between Perimeter and Semiperimeter?</h2>
  <p>The two quantities describe the same boundary at different scales, and mixing them up is one of the most frequent errors in triangle work. This section draws the line clearly.</p>

  <h3>Perimeter Measures the Full Boundary</h3>
  <p>The perimeter is the total length around the triangle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=a+b+c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>It represents the complete walk around all three edges. For the 3-4-5 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[P=12]]')} /> units.</p>

  <h3>Semiperimeter Is Half the Total</h3>
  <p>The semiperimeter is half the numerical perimeter:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>For the same 3-4-5 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} /> units. Same triangle, same boundary, but the number is halved because the formulas that consume it, Heron’s formula and the inradius relationship, are written for the half-value.</p>

  <h3>Numerical Comparison</h3>
  <p>Use a 6-8-10 right triangle. Perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[P=6+8+10]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=24}]]]')} />. Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{24}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=12}]]]')} />. The perimeter answers "how long is the whole boundary?"; the semiperimeter answers "what is the half-boundary value my formula needs?"</p>

  <h3>When Should You Use Each Measurement?</h3>
  <p>Use the perimeter when the job concerns the full boundary length: framing a triangular shape, fencing a triangular plot, estimating border materials, or measuring edge trim. Use the semiperimeter when the job is mathematical: Heron’s formula, inradius calculations, area-radius relationships, advanced geometry proofs, or independent verification of an area result. The rule of thumb is simple: physical boundary work takes <span set:html={rich('[[P]]')} />; formula work takes <span set:html={rich('[[s]].')} /></p>

  <h3>Is Semiperimeter Half the Area?</h3>
  <p>No, and this confusion needs a firm correction. Semiperimeter is a linear measurement: for 3-4-5 it is 6 units. Area is a two-dimensional measurement: for 3-4-5 it is 6 square units. The numbers coincide here by accident, but the quantities differ in kind. Halving a length never produces an area, just as halving a perimeter never produces a surface.</p>

