<!-- Section 11: Missing side -->
<section id="sec-missing-side" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Semiperimeter When One Side Is Missing</h2>
  <p>The calculator on this page requires all three side lengths: it cannot halve a total it cannot add. If one side is unknown, calculate it first with the Pythagorean theorem, then proceed with the semiperimeter as usual.</p>

  <h3>Finding the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>When both legs are known, use <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}]]]')} />. Example: <span set:html={rich('[[a=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=8]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{6^2+8^2}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{100}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=10}]]]')} />. Now the semiperimeter follows: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{6+8+10}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=12}]]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>Finding a Missing Leg</h3>
  <p>When the hypotenuse and one leg are known, use <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=\\sqrt{c^2-b^2}}]]]')} />. Example: <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]].')} /> Then <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{169-144}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{a=5}]]]')} />. Then <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{5+12+13}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=15}]]]')} />.</p>

  <h3>Why Must the Missing Side Be Found First?</h3>
  <p>Semiperimeter requires the complete total of all three sides, and an unknown side cannot simply be ignored or treated as zero. The hypotenuse form of Pythagoras adds squares; the leg form subtracts them; applying the wrong one invents a side that does not exist. Once the missing side is recovered, the <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Right Triangle Side Calculator</a> can double-check the value, and the semiperimeter calculation proceeds on solid ground. These missing-side steps are educational supporting methods, not extra inputs in the existing calculator.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Important Properties of Right Triangle Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>The semiperimeter behaves predictably. These properties double as quick mental checks and as tools in geometric reasoning.</p>

  <h3>Semiperimeter Is Greater Than the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>For every valid triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]].')} /> Adding <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> to both sides gives <span set:html={rich('[[a+b+c>2c]]')} />, and dividing by two yields <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s>c}]]]')} />. The semiperimeter always exceeds the longest side. If a calculation returns <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> smaller than <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, the labels or the arithmetic are wrong.</p>

  <h3>Semiperimeter Is Less Than the Sum of the Legs</h3>
  <p>The triangle inequality also gives <span set:html={rich('[[c<a+b]].')} /> Adding <span set:html={rich('[[a+b]]')} /> to both sides produces <span set:html={rich('[[a+b+c<2(a+b)]]')} />, and halving gives <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s<a+b}]]]')} />. So the semiperimeter sits strictly between the hypotenuse and the sum of the legs: <span set:html={rich('[[c<s<a+b]].')} /> For 3-4-5: <span set:html={rich('[[5<6<7]].')} /> This sandwich is a fast plausibility check on any answer.</p>

  <h3>Doubling Every Side Doubles Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>Scale the 3-4-5 triangle by two: <span set:html={rich('[[a_2=2a,\\;b_2=2b,\\;c_2=2c]]')} /> gives sides 6, 8, 10. Then <span set:html={rich('[[s_2=\\frac{2a+2b+2c}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s_2=2s}]]]')} />. Numerically, <span set:html={rich('[[s_2=12=2\\times6]].')} /> Semiperimeter is a linear measurement, so uniform scaling scales it by the same factor.</p>

  <h3>Tripling Every Side Triples Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>The same linearity holds for any scale factor. Triple every side and the sum triples; halving the tripled sum gives three times the original semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s_2=3s}]]]')} />. In general, scaling all sides by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> scales <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> by <span set:html={rich('[[k]].')} /> This is why similar triangles have proportional semiperimeters.</p>

  <h3>What Happens When Only One Side Changes?</h3>
  <p>Changing one side while keeping the other two fixed changes the numerical semiperimeter by half the side-length change, because only one term of the sum moves. But there is a catch: the resulting measurements must still describe a valid triangle, and for this page, a right triangle. Lengthening the hypotenuse of a 3-4-5 triangle to 6, for instance, breaks <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />, so the new numbers no longer describe a right triangle at all. Adjust one side of a right triangle and the others must adjust to preserve the right angle.</p>

  <h3>Semiperimeter of a 45-45-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Let both legs equal <span set:html={rich('[[k]].')} /> The hypotenuse is <span set:html={rich('[[c=k\\sqrt{2}]]')} />, so the semiperimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{k+k+k\\sqrt{2}}{2}]]')} />, which simplifies to <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{k(2+\\sqrt{2})}{2}}]]]')} />. The equal legs contribute symmetrically, and the irrational hypotenuse leaves its mark on the result: for <span set:html={rich('[[k=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\approx1.7071]].')} /></p>

  <h3>Semiperimeter of a 30-60-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>If the shorter leg is <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, the sides are <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[k\\sqrt{3}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[2k]].')} /> Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{k+k\\sqrt{3}+2k}{2}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{k(3+\\sqrt{3})}{2}}]]]')} />. Numerically, with <span set:html={rich('[[k=2]]')} /> the sides are 2, <span set:html={rich('[[2\\sqrt{3}\\approx3.464]]')} />, and 4, so <span set:html={rich('[[s\\approx4.732]].')} /> Exact radical form is preferable here: it keeps full precision for any later Heron or inradius computation.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Units -->
<section id="sec-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Measurement Units in Semiperimeter Calculations</h2>
  <p>Semiperimeter works in any length unit, metres, centimetres, millimetres, kilometres, feet, inches, yards, or miles, because it is just a sum of lengths, halved. The unit of the answer always matches the unit of the inputs. The only strict rule is consistency: every side must be expressed in the same unit before the addition.</p>

  <h3>Why Must All Three Side Measurements Use Compatible Units?</h3>
  <p>Semiperimeter is calculated by adding three lengths, and lengths in different units do not add directly. Adding 3 metres to 40 centimetres as if both were plain numbers gives 43 of nothing meaningful. The sum is only valid when every term shares one unit, so conversion comes before addition, never after.</p>

  <h3>Worked Unit Conversion Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ ft}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=48\\text{ inches}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Convert inches to feet first: <span set:html={rich('[[48\\text{ inches}=4\\text{ ft}]].')} /> Now all three sides are in feet: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6\\text{ ft}}]]]')} />. The measurements must be converted before addition because the formula adds raw numbers; it cannot know that 48 meant inches unless you convert it.</p>

  <h3>How Do the Calculator’s Unit Selectors Help?</h3>
  <p>Each input on this page has its own measurement-unit selector, which lets you declare the unit of the number you typed. Set each selector to match its input’s real unit so the tool combines the values on a consistent basis. Do not assume conversion features beyond what the interface shows: the selectors describe your inputs, and the semiperimeter is computed from the values as entered.</p>

  <h3>Working With Decimal Side Measurements</h3>
  <p>Decimals follow the same formula. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=2.5,\\;b=6,\\;c=6.5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{2.5+6+6.5}{2}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{15}{2}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=7.5\\text{ units}}]]]')} />. Keep full decimal precision through the addition and the division; round only the final displayed answer, and only if the problem calls for it.</p>

  <h3>Working With Irrational Side Lengths</h3>
  <p>Some right triangles have irrational sides, and the formula handles them the same way. Take a 45-45-90 triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[a=1,\\;b=1,\\;c=\\sqrt{2}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{1+1+\\sqrt{2}}{2}]]')} />, giving the exact form <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}]]]')} />, approximately <span set:html={rich('[[s\\approx1.7071]].')} /> Exact radical notation is valuable in mathematics because it preserves full precision: any later use of <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> in Heron’s formula or an inradius calculation starts from the true value, not a rounded one.</p>

  <h3>Why Should You Avoid Rounding Too Early?</h3>
  <p>Premature rounding injects error into every later calculation, and the semiperimeter is often an intermediate value, not the final answer. Rounding <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> to two decimals before feeding it into Heron’s formula, where it appears four times, compounds the error noticeably. Keep sufficient precision during intermediate steps: carry the full decimal or the exact radical, and round once, at the end, to the precision the problem requires.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: Common mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Right Triangle Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>Sixteen errors account for nearly every wrong semiperimeter. Each one is explained with what goes wrong, why it happens, and the correct approach.</p>
  <p><strong>1. Forgetting to divide the total by two.</strong> Adding 3 + 4 + 5 = 12 and stopping gives the perimeter, not the semiperimeter. Always finish with the division: <span set:html={rich('[[12/2=6]].')} /></p>
  <p><strong>2. Dividing each side before understanding the formula.</strong> Halving sides individually and then adding works arithmetically but hides the formula’s structure. Add first, divide once: <span set:html={rich('[[s=(3+4+5)/2]].')} /></p>
  <p><strong>3. Forgetting to include the hypotenuse.</strong> The hypotenuse is a boundary edge. Omitting it from a 3-4-5 triangle gives <span set:html={rich('[[(3+4)/2=3.5]]')} /> instead of 6.</p>
  <p><strong>4. Using only the two perpendicular legs.</strong> Same error as above in different clothing. The legs alone are not the whole boundary; the hypotenuse closes the shape and must be counted.</p>
  <p><strong>5. Confusing semiperimeter with full perimeter.</strong> Reporting 12 for a 3-4-5 triangle answers "what is P?" when the question asked for s. Remember: <span set:html={rich('[[s=P/2]].')} /></p>
  <p><strong>6. Confusing semiperimeter with area.</strong> For 3-4-5 both equal 6 numerically, which invites the mix-up. They differ in kind: 6 units of length versus 6 square units of surface.</p>
  <p><strong>7. Reporting the result in square units.</strong> A semiperimeter of 6 cm squared is a category error. Halving a length leaves a length: 6 cm, not 6 cm².</p>
  <p><strong>8. Using an incorrect hypotenuse.</strong> If the hypotenuse value is wrong, the sum is wrong and so is the semiperimeter. Verify <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]]')} /> when the hypotenuse was computed rather than measured.</p>
  <p><strong>9. Entering measurements that do not form a right triangle.</strong> Test <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> before trusting the label "right triangle". The addition still works, but the geometric meaning changes.</p>
  <p><strong>10. Mixing centimetres and metres.</strong> Adding 3 m to 40 cm gives neither 43 of anything. Convert to one unit first.</p>
  <p><strong>11. Mixing feet and inches.</strong> The classic imperial trap. Convert inches to feet (divide by 12) before adding, or set the unit selectors to match.</p>
  <p><strong>12. Rounding too early.</strong> Rounding <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{2}]]')} /> to 1.4 before adding accumulates error. Round the final answer only.</p>
  <p><strong>13. Using a missing side without calculating it.</strong> Two known sides are not a semiperimeter. Recover the third with the Pythagorean theorem first, then add and halve.</p>
  <p><strong>14. Applying Heron’s formula incorrectly.</strong> The factors are <span set:html={rich('[[s-a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s-b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[s-c]]')} />: each side is subtracted from the semiperimeter, not from another side. Mixing up the subtractions breaks the area.</p>
  <p><strong>15. Confusing the inradius with the circumradius.</strong> The inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r=K/s]]')} /> touches all three sides from inside; the circumradius passes through the vertices and equals <span set:html={rich('[[c/2]]')} /> for a right triangle. Different circles, different formulas.</p>
  <p><strong>16. Assuming semiperimeter describes a physical half-boundary path.</strong> It is half the numerical total, not a marked halfway route around the triangle. Treat it as the derived intermediate value it is.</p>
  <p><strong>Wrong versus correct, side by side.</strong> Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3,\\;b=4,\\;c=5]]')} />: incorrect is <span set:html={rich('[[s=3+4+5=12]]')} />, which skips the division and reports the full perimeter. Also incorrect is <span set:html={rich('[[s=(3+4)/2=3.5]]')} />, which omits the hypotenuse. Correct is <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{3+4+5}{2}]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6}]]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Semiperimeter Answer Is Correct</h2>
  <p>Eight independent checks catch almost every error. The 3-4-5 triangle demonstrates each one. Independent checks matter because they fail in different ways: when unrelated methods agree, the answer has earned its trust.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 1: Double the semiperimeter.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{P=2s}]]]')} /> must equal the full perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[2\\times6=12=3+4+5]].')} /> If doubling does not reproduce the side sum, the division or the addition went wrong.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 2: Recalculate from all three sides.</strong> Recompute <span set:html={rich('[[s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}]]')} /> from scratch: <span set:html={rich('[[(3+4+5)/2=6]].')} /> A second pass catches keystroke and arithmetic slips.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 3: Verify the Pythagorean relationship.</strong> Check <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]].')} /> This confirms the sides form a right triangle, which validates the side labels the semiperimeter depends on.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 4: Check the hypotenuse.</strong> Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[c>a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c>b]]')} />: 5 is greater than 3 and greater than 4. If the supposed hypotenuse is not the longest side, the labels are wrong.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 5: Check the triangle inequality.</strong> Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a+b>c]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[3+4=7>5]].')} /> The sum of the legs must exceed the hypotenuse, or no triangle exists.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 6: Verify the semiperimeter bound.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s>c}]]]')} /> must hold: <span set:html={rich('[[6>5]].')} /> This single inequality catches a surprising number of labelling errors.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 7: Check the units.</strong> Confirm that all three side lengths use compatible units and that the output is a linear measurement. Mixed units invalidate the sum even when the arithmetic is perfect.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 8: Compare with the calculator’s solution steps.</strong> The displayed result and calculation steps help identify mistakes: if a step shows a mistyped side or a skipped division, the error is visible before the answer is used.</p>
</section>

