  <h2>Half the Boundary, Full Understanding</h2>
  <p>The semiperimeter of a right triangle is a small idea with a long reach. One addition and one division produce a number that unlocks the inradius, powers Heron’s formula, and sharpens area verification. The formula asks for three sides because the boundary has three edges, and the hypotenuse earns its place in the sum as the edge that closes the shape. Everything else in this guide, the worked examples, the twelve applications, the properties, the unit conversions, the sixteen mistakes, the eight verification checks, exists to protect that one halved total from the small labelling and unit errors that produce plausible-looking wrong answers.</p>
  <p>Use this page’s calculator whenever all three sides are measured, recover a missing side through the Pythagorean theorem when they are not, and check the result against the bounds c &lt; s &lt; a + b before the number goes into a drawing or a model. When the question turns to the surface inside the boundary, Heron’s formula and the area calculator are waiting; when it turns to the circle inside the triangle, the inradius is one division away.</p>
</section>
